      <h1>OpenTelemetry vs Prometheus: Not Rivals, Different Layers</h1>

      <div class="not-prose my-8 rounded-lg border border-indigo-200 bg-indigo-50/60 p-6 dark:border-indigo-500/30 dark:bg-indigo-500/5">
        <p class="mb-0 text-indigo-900/90 dark:text-indigo-200/90"><strong>OpenTelemetry vs Prometheus isn't really an either/or choice: OpenTelemetry is how your app <em>produces and ships</em> telemetry, and Prometheus is a system that <em>stores, queries and alerts on</em> metrics.</strong> They overlap in one place, metrics instrumentation and collection, and that's where you have real decisions to make. This guide explains the layers, then wires up a Node.js app three ways and shows the gotchas we hit.</p>
      </div>

      <blockquote>
        <p><strong>Tested with</strong> Prometheus 3.15.0, OpenTelemetry Collector Contrib 0.162.0, the OpenTelemetry JS SDK 0.223.0 and prom-client 15.1.3 on Node 22.23.3 with Express 5.3.0 (2026-10-15). Every config and snippet below ran on our test machine, and the outputs are real. devxtools.dev is independent. Both projects are open source under the CNCF, and nobody sponsored this page.</p>
      </blockquote>

      <h2 id="tldr">TL;DR</h2>

      <p>OpenTelemetry is a vendor-neutral standard and toolkit for producing and shipping traces, metrics and logs. Prometheus is a metrics database and monitoring system that stores, queries and alerts on metrics. They're complementary: many teams instrument with OpenTelemetry and send the metrics to Prometheus.</p>

      <p><strong>Quick decision:</strong> if you only need metrics and dashboards for a Node service, <code>prom-client</code> plus Prometheus is the simplest setup. If you also want traces, or want to keep the option of switching backends, instrument with OpenTelemetry and send metrics to Prometheus, either scraped or over OTLP.</p>

      <h2 id="what-each-one-is">What each one is</h2>

      <p><strong><a href="/tools/opentelemetry/">OpenTelemetry</a> (OTel)</strong> is a specification plus implementations:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>APIs and SDKs</strong> for each language (in Node: <code>@opentelemetry/api</code>, <code>@opentelemetry/sdk-node</code>) to create spans, metrics and logs.</li>
        <li><strong>Auto-instrumentation</strong> (<code>@opentelemetry/auto-instrumentations-node</code>) that instruments HTTP, Express, database clients and more without code changes.</li>
        <li><strong>OTLP</strong>, the wire protocol (gRPC on 4317, HTTP on 4318) that most observability backends now accept.</li>
        <li><strong>The Collector</strong>, a standalone agent/gateway with <em>receivers</em> (OTLP, Prometheus scraping and many others), <em>processors</em> (batching, filtering, sampling, attribute changes) and <em>exporters</em> (Prometheus, remote write, OTLP to vendors).</li>
      </ul>

      <p>OTel has <strong>no storage, no query language, no dashboards and no alerting</strong>. It always exports to something else.</p>

      <p><strong>Prometheus</strong> is a monitoring system:</p>

      <ul>
        <li>A <strong>time-series database</strong> for metrics.</li>
        <li>A <strong>scraper</strong> that pulls <code>/metrics</code> endpoints on an interval (the pull model).</li>
        <li><strong>PromQL</strong> for queries, plus <strong>alerting rules</strong> that fire into <strong>Alertmanager</strong>.</li>
        <li>An ecosystem of <strong>exporters</strong> (node_exporter and so on) and <strong>client libraries</strong> (for Node, <code>prom-client</code>).</li>
        <li><strong>Remote write</strong> for long-term or scaled storage (Mimir, Thanos, Cortex), and since Prometheus 3.x an optional <strong>OTLP receiver</strong>.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>Prometheus handles <strong>metrics only</strong>: no traces, no logs.</p>

      <h2 id="at-a-glance">At a glance</h2>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
